tasks.description:
validate deliberately does NOT execute the migration's own BEGIN/COMMIT - it records them as no-ops and runs each statement in its own transaction (internal/validate/capture.go), and it hoists CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY out of any transaction entirely. So this migration:
    BEGIN;
    CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx ON orders (user_id);
    COMMIT;
applies cleanly in rowshape and returns PASS - while in production, under every runner that wraps a file in one transaction (Rails, Django, golang-migrate, Flyway), it raises 25001 'CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY cannot run inside a transaction block'. The same holds for ALTER TYPE ... ADD VALUE pre-12, VACUUM, REINDEX CONCURRENTLY and CREATE DATABASE. VERIFIED: the probe returns PASS with zero findings. This is the worst class of miss in the catalog because the sandbox does not merely fail to notice a hazard - it ACTIVELY DIVERGES from production semantics in a way that manufactures confidence. Statement.Concurrent is already a captured field, so the information needed to detect it is present and unused.
